coordination bonds and a more stable framework. Group 4 metal cations generally exist in the oxidation state of $+4_{,}$ , which is expected to form stable MOFs when combined with carboxylate-based ligands.19,20 In addition to the stronger metal−ligand bonds, these $^{4+}$ metal cations require more ligands to balance their charge. Therefore, their inorganic nodes tend to have high connectivity, which, to some extent, enhances the stability of the framework by preventing the attack by guests such as water molecules.
Compared to the well-studied $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}.$ - and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based MOFs, group 4 metal-based MOFs $\mathrm{(M^{IV}-M O F s)}$ are a relatively new research field. The first $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF was reported in $\dot{2}008^{19}$ followed by the discovery of Ti-MOF in 2009.20 Nevertheless, they have drawn increasing attention since their discovery particularly because of their high stability and wide application scope. Previous reviews have provided comprehensive summaries of $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - and Ti-based MOFs, including their syntheses, structures, properties, and applications.18,21−23 This Outlook will not repeat these reviews but will briefly introduce the history of $\begin{array}{r}{\bar{\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}.}\end{array}$ -MOFs, highlight their unique properties, and explore the current challenges and emerging opportunities in this area.
## A BRIEF HISTORY
Zr-MOFs. The first $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF, UiO-66, was reported in 2008 (UiO stands for University of Oslo).19 It is constructed by linking 12-connected $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O H})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}\right]$ clusters (Figure 2a,b) with linear BDC linkers into a network with fcu topology (Figure 2e). It possesses two types of micropores, tetrahedral and octahedral (Figure 2f), and a BET surface area close to $1200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Isoreticular expansion of UiO-66 with elongated linkers successfully generated two isostructures, namely, UiO-67 and UiO-68. After this publication, there were few reports on new $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs until 2011, aside from some works exploring the functionalization and applications of UiOtype structures.24−27 The discovery of new $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs is largely hindered by synthetic difficulties. The strong $z\mathrm{r}{-}\mathrm{O}$ bonds slow down the ligand exchange rate and thus inhibit the crystallization process. Direct synthesis of stable MOFs using a mixture of metal salts and organic linkers usually leads to quick precipitation of low crystalline powders. In fact, the UiO series were initially obtained as polycrystalline powders. Without single crystals, their structures need to be determined by complicated Rietveld refinements against synchrotron powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data, which further impedes the discovery new $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs.
In 2011, this problem was overcome by modulated synthesis, using terminal carboxylic acids as modulators.28 The terminal carboxylic acids competitively coordinate with the metals and suppress the deprotonation of linkers. As a result, nucleation and crystal growth are slowed to allow for the formation of highly crystalline products. Using benzoic acid as modulator, single crystals of UiO- ${\cdot68\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ were obtained, providing the first single-crystal structure of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs. Modulated synthesis has proved effective for most $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs and thus has dramatically accelerated the discovery of new structures.18,29,30 According to the Cambridge Structural Database $\left(\mathrm{CSD}\right),^{31}$ about $200\ {\mathrm{Zr}}.$ - MOFs have been recorded so far.
A unique feature of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster is the capability to vary the connection number without altering the robust $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O)}_{4}(\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH})_{4}]$ core. Besides the aforementioned 12-connected $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}\right]$ clusters,19 11-connected $\mathrm{\small{[Zr}}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{O)}}_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{OH}})_{4}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathrm{O)}}({\mathrm{COO}})_{11}\],^{32}$ 10-connected $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{10}],^{33}$ 8-connected $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{8}],^{34}$ and 6-connected [Zr6(μ3-O)4(μ3-OH)4(OH)6(H2O)6(COO)6]30,35,36 clusters have also been documented in which each missing carboxylate was replaced by a terminal monocarboxylate ligand or a pair of ${-}\mathrm{OH}^{-}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to balance the charge. Note that the proton assignments for these clusters with $-\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}$ groups are not clear, although simulations indicated that the $-\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are interconvertible by rapid proton transfers.37
This variable connection number further enriches the structural abundance of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs. Meanwhile, the maintained $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}\right]$ core facilitates structural prediction. As an example, the possible structures formed by $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters and tetracarboxylate linkers can be predicted by a reverse topological approach.38 First, possible geometries of organic linkers and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters are analyzed: the tetratopic linkers are simplified into square planar nodes, whereas $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters can be represented by 12-connected cuboctahedrons, 8-connected tetragonal prisms, 6-connected hexagons, or 6-connected triangular antiprisms. Possible topologies formed by the combination of two nodes were then listed including ftw, csq, scu, sqc, and she. These topologies were used as templates to construct hypothetical MOFs by replacing the topological nodes with $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters and organic linkers. Finally, the predicted structures were carefully examined, geometrically optimized, and experimentally synthesized. Guided by topological analysis, a series of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs based on a 4-connected linker, TCPP, were constructed, including MOF-525 (ftw),39 PCN-222 (csq),34 NU-902 (scu),40 PCN-224 (she),36 and $\mathrm{PCN}{-}225(\mathbf{sqc})^{41}$ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}T C P P}\ =$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)- porphyrin; NU stands for Northwestern University; PCN stands for porous coordination network). Using the same strategy, the Snurr group has predicted 204 hypothetical $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOFs and examined their performance in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption, 38 which guided the syntheses of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs for targeted applications.
Besides the $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}]$ core, $Z\mathbf{r}$ -clusters featuring other core structures have also been occasionally observed in MOFs. Polymeric double chains were found in MIL-140 (MIL stands for Materials of Institut Lavoisier) which was composed of edge sharing $\mathrm{ZrO}_{7}$ polyhedra connected through linear ligands.42 The 1D building unit has also been observed in CAU-22 (CAU stands for Christian-Albrechts-Universität), which is perceived as 6-connected $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters bridged by $\mu_{2^{-}}$ OH.43 Recently, the $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{12}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{8}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{6}(\mathrm{COO})_{18}\right]$ cluster was discovered, which can be viewed as a pair of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters linked by $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ groups. $^{44-46}$ Note that most $Z\mathbf{r}.$ MOFs have Hf-based analogues. $^{33,35,47}$ Due to lanthanide contraction, ${\mathrm{Hf}}^{4+}$ has the same ${\bf\bar{d}}^{0}$ electronic configuration and a nearly identical radius as ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ . Therefore, the Hf-MOFs usually show identical structures and properties as their $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based counterparts, except for the formula weight. Considering the diversity of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -clusters and the ever-increasing research interest in stable MOFs, the number of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs will continuously grow in the near future.
Ti-MOFs. Ti-MOFs possess several advantages over $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOFs such as high photocatalytic activities, earth abundance, and relatively low toxicity.48 The first Ti-MOF, MIL-125, was reported in 2009, one year after the discovery of Zr-MOFs.20 It is constructed from 12-connected $[\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(C O O)_{12}}]$ clusters (Figure 2c) and BDC linkers affording a fcu network (Figure $2\mathrm{g},$ ). It has the same topology as the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF, UiO-66, although the $\left[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}\right]$ cluster is less symmetric than the $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}\right]$ cluster. It demonstrates permanent porosity with a BET surface area of 1550 $\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .20 The amine functionalized BDC linker also forms the MIL-125 structure.49,50 One would expect that isostructural TiMOFs can be obtained with longer linear linkers such as BPDC (BPDC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate). However, the synthesis of MIL-125 isostructures using elongated linkers was carried out but to no avail.
Inspired by the success of modulated synthesis of $\mathbf{Zr{-}M O F s},$ the modulated synthesis of Ti-MOFs was attempted by our group using a $\mathrm{\small{[Ti_{6}O_{6}(O M e)_{6}(A B Z)_{6}]}}$ cluster as the metal resource and carboxylic acids as modulators, in expectation of a $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}$ -based MOF $\left({\tt A B Z}=4\right.$ -aminobenzoate). However, despite the similarity between $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , the modulated synthesis of Ti-MOFs was not successful. The $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}$ -cluster tends to reassemble during MOF synthesis.51 Recent developments in synthetic technique have partially overcome this difficulty by combining the synthetic strategies of MOFs and covalent organic frameworks (COFs).52 In this method, the same hexameric Ti-oxo-cluster with amino groups, $\big[\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}\big(\mathrm{OMe}\big)_{6}.$ $(\mathrm{ABZ})_{6}]$ , was in situ generated and further extended into a 2D layer by dialdehyde spacers through an imine condensation reaction.53 Two Ti-MOFs, namely, MOF-901 and MOF-902, are successfully obtained.53,54 They represent a combination of MOFs and COFs with both coordination bonds and dynamic covalent bonds. The formations of both $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}$ -clusters and imine bonds are favored under the synthetic conditions, although it is not clear which bond is the labile one that controls the crystallization process.
Until now, only a limited number of Ti-carboxylate-based coordination polymers (MIL-125 and its derivatives,20 PCN22,51 COK-69,55 MOF-901,53 MOF-902,54 NTU-9,56 MIL-167, MIL-168, and MIL- $169^{57}$ ) have been reported (NTU stands for Nanyang Technological University). Among all these coordination polymers, permanent porosities were proven for MIL125, PCN-22, MOF-901, and MOF-902. Different clusters were observed in each of these MOFs $\left[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\right]$ for MIL-125, $[\mathrm{Ti}_{7}]$ for PCN-22, and $\left[\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\right]$ for MOF-901/902). Compared with the well-established synthetic method for $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs, there is still a lack of an efficient way to take control over the formation of TiMOFs.
Ti−Zr Bimetallic MOFs. The synthetic difficulties of TiMOFs are attributed to the incompatible formation condition of Ti-clusters with those required for MOF synthesis. In contrast, the robust $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{O}\bar{\mathrm{H}})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}]$ cluster affords the formation of various Zr-MOFs via modulated synthesis.18,19 The combination of Ti and $Z\mathrm{r}$ in a bimetallic $[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}-$ $\left(\mathrm{COO}\right)_{16}]$ cluster has brought new opportunities in the synthesis of Ti-containing MOFs. Starting from $[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}-$ $\left(\mathrm{\'MeCOO}\right)_{16}]$ clusters and linear linkers with different lengths and functional groups, a series of isoreticular bimetallic MOFs, the PCN-415 and PCN-416 series, were synthesized under synthetic conditions similar to those used for Zr-MOFs.58
The robust $\left[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}(\mathrm{COO})_{16}\right]$ clusters were maintained in the resulting MOFs. The decanuclear $\left[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}(\mathrm{COO})_{16}\right]$ cluster is composed of a $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ -cube capped by two ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ centers on the top and bottom (Figure 2d). The $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ were further bridged by four $\mu_{2}{\bf-O},$ , eight $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O},$ and 16 carboxylates generating the $\left[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}(\mathrm{COO})_{16}\right]$ cluster. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{\small{[Ti_{8}Z r_{2}O_{12}(C O O)_{16}]}}$ cluster can be regarded as a derivative of the $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(C O O)_{12}]}}$ cluster by replacing four equatorial ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ by a $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ -cube. As a result, PCN-415 and PCN-416 share the same topology as their $\mathrm{z}\mathbf{r}$ -analogues, UiO-66 (Figure 2h). Both PCN-415 and PCN-416 are highly porous with BET surface areas of 1050 and $1337~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. They also demonstrate excellent chemical stability, tunable photoresponse, and good activity toward photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reactions. Considering the synthetic difficulties of Ti-MOFs, the $[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}(\mathrm{COO}\bar{)}_{16}]$ cluster is believed to be a Ti-containing replacement of the $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(C O O)_{12}]}}$ cluster, which promises the construction of various robust and photoactive MOFs.
By comparing the history of Ti-MOFs and $\mathbf{Zr{-}M O F s},$ , some useful information can be provided. The field of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs has developed rapidly since 2011 when an effective synthetic method was developed. Modulated synthesis allows for the facile production of high quality MOF materials, which contributes to the success of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs.59 Over the past few years, $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF UiO-66 has almost dethroned MOF-5 and HKUST-1 (HKUST stands for Hong Kong University of Science and Technology) as a benchmark MOF material. Unfortunately, the Ti-MOF field remained relatively silent for almost 10 years with sporadic reports of new structures. Once the synthetic challenges are overcome, we believe that new TiMOFs will begin to burgeon. On the other hand, the discovery of $\mathrm{Ti-Zr}$ bimetallic clusters may offer an opportunity for the development of new Ti-containing photoactive MOFs.58
## STABILITY
The outstanding chemical stability makes ${\bf M}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ -MOFs an attractive reasearch area. It is necessary to introduce the fundamentals of MOF stability in a tutorial manner in order to understand the stability of ${\bf M}^{\mathrm{I\dot{V}}}{-}\mathrm{MOFs}$ . The stability of MOFs is affected by multiple factors, including the metal nodes, organic ligands, metal−ligand coordination, operating environment, hydrophobicity of the pore surface, defects, etc.14−16 The metal−ligand bond strength is an important factor that determines the thermodynamic stability of MOFs. Since it is not always easy to quantify the bond strength, the stability of MOFs can be roughly compared according to the Pearson’s hard/soft acid/base principle.17 Strong coordination bonds are expected between hard Lewis acids and bases4,19 or soft Lewis acids and bases.60−62 Carboxylate-based ligands can be regarded as hard bases, which form stable MOFs with group 4 metal cations. Besides the influence of bond strength that determines thermodynamic stability, the stability of MOFs can also be affected by kinetic factors. Dense and rigid framework structures formed by highly connected metal-oxo clusters and rigid organic linkers enhance the overall stability of MOFs. This also partially explains the good stability of $\mathbf{M}^{4+}$ -based MOFs, because $\mathbf{M}^{4+}$ cations require more ligands to balance their charge.
Since it is not always easy to quantify the bond strength, the stability of MOFs can be roughly compared according to the Pearson’s hard/soft acid/base principle.
The stability of a MOF also dependents on the operating environments. Generally, MOFs constructed from high-valent metal ions and carboxylate-based ligands exhibit excellent stability in acids, while their resistance to base is weak. In acid, the degradation of MOFs is mainly caused by the competition of proton and metal ion for the coordinating linkers (Scheme 1).63 The low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of carboxylic acids and strong coordination bonds endow these MOFs with excellent stability in acids. However, the strong affinity between high-valence metal cations and $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ facilitates the replacement of linkers by $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ , which results in their lability toward basic solutions. The $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF UiO-66, Ti-MOF MIL-125, and $\mathrm{Ti-Zr}$ bimetallic MOF PCN-415 show similar stability in aqueous solution from $\mathrm{\pH}=1$ to $\mathrm{pH}=12.$ , indicating a better stability in acid than in base. Note that different synthetic procedures may give rise to MOFs with different defects and particle sizes, which may also affect the stability. Another extreme example is $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF PCN
222 (also known as MOF-545) constructed from ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ and carboxylate linkers.34,39 It can maintain its crystallinity in concentrated HCl $(12\textbf{M})$ but readily decomposes in dilute $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ solutions $(0.1~\mathrm{\mM})$ . Therefore, ${{\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{{W}}}}$ -MOFs can find application in acidic environments, but one should be cautious when using them under basic conditions.
## DEFECTS
It has been proven that defects exist in almost any MOFs, such as MOF-5 and HKUST-1.64−68 However, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs have attracted particular attention because of their remarkable tolerance toward a high concentration of defects without suffering from severe loss of crystallinity or stability.69,70 This is related to the high connectivity of $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters (Figure 3a) and their capability to reduce their connection number. Take the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF, UiO-66 as an example (Figure $^{3\mathrm{b,d}},$ , two types of defects were observed: missing-linkers and missing-clusters.71
The missing linker defect is generated by the removal of an organic linker from a pair of adjacent $Z\mathrm{r}$ -clusters, leaving coordination vacancies.70 The coordination vacancy on the $Z\mathbf{r}.$ cluster is believed to be terminated by a monocarboxylate or a pair of ${-\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}}$ to balance the charge.72 Structural evidence of the missing linker defects in UiO-66 is provided by neutron powder diffraction in which the defects are described by a linker occupancy of 0.917, corresponding to an $8.3\%$ missing linker vacancy.72 Single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) allows further insight into the defect sites. The replacement of carboxylate by terminal ${-\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}}$ was proven by the electron densities around the carboxylate oxygens.73,74
The pore size distributions derived from $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of UiO-66 indicate the existence of large pores with a diameter of $1.7~\mathrm{nm},^{75,76}$ ,75,76 which cannot be explained by missing linker defects. This is attributed to the missing cluster defect, which results from the removal of a $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster, along with the 12 surrounding linkers (Figure 3c). Intuitively, the missing linker defects and missing cluster defects are believed to be randomly distributed throughout the MOF particle.71 However, synchrotron PXRD in combination with pair distribution function (PDF) analysis, anomalous $\mathrm{x}$ -ray scattering, and electron diffraction suggested a secondary crystalline phase formed by the missing cluster defects. In this case, a framework with reo topology is locally created and exists in the form of nanodomains, in which each cluster is only 8-connected (Figure 3c,e). Later, similar missing linker defects and missing cluster defects have been observed in other UiO-type structures with elongated linkers.77−79
Tuning MOF Properties by Defect Engineering. As many properties are strongly affected by defects, it is possible to tailor the performance of MOFs by defect engineering .68,69 The concentration of defects can be affected by synthetic conditions including the metal to ligand ratio, type of modulators, modulator concentration, reaction temperature, and so on.72,80,81 Generally, a less defective MOF can be generated with low metal to ligand ratios at high temperature.80 However, defects that can be created in a one-pot reactions are still limited, because highly defective structures with relatively low stability cannot survive harsh solvothermal synthetic conditions.
Linker labilization was developed to create a high concentration of defects in $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs in a stepwise manner.78 A defect free $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF was initially synthesized with a mixture of stable linker and imine-based pro-labile linker. The pro-labile linkers are subsequently labilized by breaking the imine bond under acidic conditions to introduce missing linker defects. Taking advantage of the acid stability of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -clusters, the crystallinity of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs can be well maintained after the removal of labile linkers. This method allows controlled generation of defects in $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs by changing the labile linker ratio and acid concentration. Later, this method was extended using thermolabile linkers instead of acid-labile linkers, which generates defects by thermal decomposition of linkers.82,83 A direct consequence of defects is the increased maximum pore volume and BET surface areas as a result of the expanded cavity and decreased material density.
Besides the increase of pore size and surface area, the defects also endow additional functions to the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs. The defect sites of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs were found to be active Lewis acidic sites for catalytic reactions, including cross-aldol condensation reactions,84 ring-opening of epoxides,47 Meerwein−Ponndorf− Verley reactions,85 and the isomerization of $\alpha$ -pinene oxide.86 The Lewis acidity as well as the catalytic performance can be improved by defect engineering. For example, Vermoortele et al. used trifluoroacetic acid and HCl in the synthesis of UiO-66 to induce more defects as Lewis acidic sites after thermal activation.87 In another example, the degradation of chemical warfare agents by $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs has been investigated. Hupp and co-workers have shown $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs, NU-1000, can hydrolyze organophosphate-based nerve agents and simulants because of their Lewis acidity.88−96 The nerve agent simulant dimethyl 4- nitrophenyl phosphate is believed to bind to NU-1000 by hydrogen bonding with ${-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{OH}^{-}}$ groups on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -nodes as well as by weak $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions with the benzene ring of the organic linkers. The $\mathrm{z}\mathbf{r}$ -clusters with low connection numbers contains more $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{z}r\mathrm{-}O H}$ and $\mathrm{Zr-OH}_{2},$ leading to increased hydrolysis rates.89,94,96
Functionalizing MOFs by Defect Modification. The existence of defects dramatically enhanced the versatility of $Z\mathbf{r}\mathbf{.}$ MOFs, as the coordination vacancy provides a chemical handle for postsynthetic modifications. MOFs featuring low-connected $Z\mathbf{r}$ -clusters have been well-documented in which the connection number can be 11, 10, 8, or 6. These structures can be regarded as “intrinsically defective” MOFs with inherent coordination vacancies arranged in a crystallographically ordered manner. Taking advantage of these structures, a series of unique postsynthetic modification strategies have been developed, including linker incorporation and cluster metalation.97,98
Ligand Incorporation. It was observed that the terminal ligands on the coordinatively unsaturated $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{\tilde{OH})_{4}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}(C O O)_{8}]}$ clusters (Figure 4b,e) can be replaced postsynthetically. This inspires the development of the solvent-assisted ligand incorporation (SALI) method by replacing the terminal ligands on the $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}\right]$ - $(\mathrm{\tilde{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{\check{H}}_{2}O)_{4}(C O O)_{8}]}$ clusters with a different monocarboxylates, phosphates, and sulfates.99,100 For example, different perfluoroalkyl carboxylate entities were attached to the clusters of NU-1000 to enhance $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake (Figure $^{4\mathrm{h},\mathrm{g}^{\cdot}}$ .99 The postsynthetically attached ligands are believed to coordinate to the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters by replace the ${-}\mathrm{OH}^{-}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ pairs (Figure $^{4\mathrm{a},\mathrm{d}}$ ). Linker installation is an elegant example that takes advantage of the missing linker vacancy and postsynthetically installs linear linkers in the vacancy. A $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF, PCN-700, with 8-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters and multiple missing linker sites at determined positions was constructed, and linkers with different lengths were sequentially installed at different defect sites.32 Systematic variation of the pore volume and decoration of pore environment were realized by sequential installation of multiple linkers with different lengths or functional groups, which provides an effective method to functionalize $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs.101 It was later demonstrated that the defect sites in UiO-66 and UiO-67 can be postsynthetically grafted with a monocarboxylic species. The missing linker defects in UiO-67 can also be “healed” postsynthetically by inserting a BPDC linker,79 a process similar to linker installation. These examples imply that the modification of defect sites by ligands can be a general method to functionalize ${\bf{Z r{-}M O F s}}$ .
Cluster Metalation. The $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster acts as an acid when reacting with carboxylates. Considering its amphoteric behavior, it is not surprising that the $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}(}\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O)}_{4}(\bar{\mu}_{3}–\mathrm{OH})_{4}-$ $\mathrm{(OH)}_{4}\mathrm{(H}_{2}O\mathrm{)}_{4}\mathrm{(COO)}_{8}\mathrm{\bar{]}}$ clusters can also bind to metal cations as a base (Figure $\mathsf{4c}),$ . In fact, it has been reported that molecular $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters can bind with a heterometal through the bridging $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ or $\mu_{3}–0$ .102 In a $\mathbf{Zr\mathrm{-}M O F}.$ , the heterometals can be deposited on the coordinatively unsaturated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -cluster through gas-phase or liquid-phase methods.
The gas-phase immobilization of heterometals reassembles the atomic layer deposition process which requires organometallic reagents or metal salts as metal precursors.103−105 For example, exposing the NU-1000 samples in $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Et})_{2}$ or $\mathsf{A l}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Me}})_{3}$ vapor led to the metalation of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster by $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ , respectively (Figure 4f,i).103 This is a close remittance to the hydroxylated metal-oxide surface where surface $-\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{-OH}_{2}$ bind to metal precursors. Later, this method was extended to liquid-phase by directly reacting MOF with a solution of metal salts.106 Compared with the gas-phase deposition, the liquid-phase method can be easily applied to a wider range of metal precursors. This method was systematically explored by the Hupp group using NU-1000 as a platform, generating a series of metal clusters or metal− organic complex-based catalysts.97,107 Definitive evidence of the positions and coordination environments of the incorporated metal ions was provided by SCXRD using single crystalline PCN-700 as a platform.108 Note that coordination environment of postsynthetically incorporated metal can be more complicated in other MOF systems such as NU-1000. The metals can be disordered, partially occupied, or form multinuclear clusters around the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters. The metalation of clusters does not occur exclusively on low-connected $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - clusters. In fact, the $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ on the 12-connected $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}]$ clusters can also be deprotonated by a strong base such as $\left[\mathrm{AuMe}(\mathrm{PMe}_{3})\right].$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{MgBr}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ ${\mathrm{{}^{t}B u O L i,}}$ or $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{Mg},$ leading to the replacement of protons by metal cations.109,110 Lin and co-workers use this method to stabilize a single-site $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ -alkyl catalyst for the ketone hydroboration in UiO-type MOFs with 12-connected clusters.111
The defect sites can also be coordinated by metallates such as molybdates, tungstates, vanadate, chromates, and arsenate s.112−114 For example, UiO-66 has been modified with vanadate as a catalyst for olefin epoxidation.114 Defective $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOFs have also been used to capture chromates and arsenates from water.113,115 Although this behavior looks similar to the incorporation of low-valent transition metals, they are essentially different. The $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters act as acids, whereas the metallates act as bases. This process can be regarded as an analogue of ligand incorporation.
Modification of defects is a valuable addition to the existing toolbox of postsynthetic methods116,117 to functionalize $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOFs. Considering the high chemical stability of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs and the abundant functional groups/metals that can be postsynthetically incorporated, it is apparent that unlimited potential exists by the modification of defective $\mathrm{Zr}$ -clusters for applications in gas storage, water purification, and catalysis.
Photocatalytic Properties. The high chemical stability of ${{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ -MOFs offers a wide scope of potential applications, especially under harsh conditions. Besides their conventional role in gas storage, ${\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{IV}}{\mathbf{-MOFs}}$ find emerging applications in Lewis acid catalysis, Brønsted acid catalysis, redox catalysis, photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, sensing, and biomedicine.18,118−120 For example, ${\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ -MOFs have been studied for air purification of toxic gases such as ammonia, carbon monoxide, nitrogen oxides, sulfur-containing gases, etc.121−123 Recently, ${\bf M}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ -MOFs have also shown promising performances in catalytic degradation of chemical warfare agents such as sarin and sulfur mustard.92,94,124 These applications have been well discussed in some comprehensive reviews.125−128 We do not intend to cover the great number of publications in all these areas. Instead, we will focus the photocatalysis as a representative example that takes advantage of the high chemical stability, ${\bf d}^{\hat{0}}$ configuration, and unique photoredox activity of group 4 metal cations.
MOFs represent an array of nanosized metal-oxide clusters periodically arranged within the crystal lattice. Many metaloxide semiconductors are known as heterogeneous photocatalysts for hydrogen generation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and organic transformations. One representative example is ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ which is considered as one of the most successful photocatalysts due to its high conductivity, efficient charge separations, chemical stability, earth abundance, and relatively low toxicity.129,130 As an analogue of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles, Ti-MOFs with nanosized Ti-oxo clusters have been explored for their potential applications in photocatalysis. Upon the discovery of the first Ti-MOF MIL-125, they have been studied as a photocatalysts. Reversible photochromic behavior in MIL-125 was observed upon UV irradiation in alcohol, indicating the photoinduced generation of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ species.20 Later, MIL-125 and aminofunctionalized MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\protect$ ) have been studied as photocatalysts for hydrogen generation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and decomposition of organic dyes.131−133 The presence of electron donating $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups contributes to the light absorption in the visible light region and facilitates the ligand to cluster charge transfer.50 UiO-66 and the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -functionalized analogue $\big(\mathrm{Ui}\bar{\mathrm{O}}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ also show photocatalytic activity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.25,134 In most cases, sacrificial donors and cocatalysts such as Pt nanoparticles are used to assist hydrogen production.
MOFs represent an array of nanosized metal-oxide clusters periodically arranged within the crystal lattice.
While the visible light absorption of ${\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ is still limited, porphyrin-based linkers can significantly increase the light absorption efficiency of MOFs. In fact, porphyrin-based MOFs with metal-oxo clusters connected to porphyrin sensitizers are inherent dye sensitized photocatalytic systems that potentially mimics porphyrin-sensitized metal oxides. Our group integrated the Ti-cluster and photosensitizing porphyrinic linker into a MOF named PCN-22.51 As a proof of concept, PCN-22 was employed as a photocatalyst for an alcohol oxidation reaction. Later, the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -porphyrin MOFs, PCN-222 (also known as MOF-545) and MOF-525, were also investigated as a photocatalyst for CO2 reduction.135
Compared with inorganic semiconductors, MOFs provide additional opportunities to control the light-absorption properties by functionalizing the organic linkers. The introduction of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ substituted linkers or specific chromophores can effectively enhance the photoresponse of MOFs. The porous structure makes each single metal-oxide cluster in MOFs accessible by the substrates, which facilitates the catalytic reaction. However, although the optical band-gaps of MOFs are similar to metal-oxide semiconductors, the located electronic states indicate that they are mostly insulators. The low conductivity of MOFs inevitably limits their applications especially in photoelectrochemical devices. So far, studies of MOFs in photoelectrocatalysis mainly focus on either oxidation or reduction half reactions, and their performances are still far from competing with other state of the art systems.
## OUTLOOK
With escalated research interest in stable MOFs, the field of ${{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ -MOFs has been flourishing in the past few years. An overview of the history of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs and Ti-MOFs suggests that the bottleneck of Ti-MOFs lies in the lack of a general synthetic method. The discovery of $\mathrm{Ti-Zr}$ bimetallic clusters provides a way to overcome these synthetic difficulties. A new class of Ti− $Z\mathbf{r}$ bimetallic MOFs is envisioned, and their applications await exploration.
The high concentration of defects in Zr-MOFs offers an additional opportunity to incorporate functional groups in the defect sites.
As a unique feature of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF, defects are discussed with an emphasis on their effect on MOF properties. The high concentration of defects in $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs offers an additional opportunity to incorporate functional groups in the defect sites. We introduced two methods to tether organic linkers or metals on the defect sites and their possible applications. These examples are not exhaustive, but represent the vast opportunities to functionalize $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF for specialized applications by the modification of $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters. Considering the similarity between Ti and $Z\mathbf{r}$ , defects are expected to exist in Ti-MOFs and $\mathrm{Ti-Zr}$ bimetallic MOFs.58 The Ti-MOFs and $\mathrm{Ti-Zr}$ bimetallic MOFs provide suitable platforms for the study of defects and their related applications. We anticipate that this research area has the potential to be greatly expanded, resulting in a new class of robust MOF materials with desired functionalities.
